== 8 The integral conclusion and motivic consequences <sec:conclusion>

=== 8.1 The integral zero-cycle theorem <subsec:integral-proof>

Both numerical branches now supply the nonzero coefficient required by Corollary 25. We assemble the rational vanishing and then use Roitman’s theorem to remove torsion.

#proof[
  For a surface of Kodaira dimension below two, the assertion follows from Bloch–Kas–Lieberman as in Proposition 5. For a surface of general type, pass to a smooth minimal model $X_0$. If $K_(X_0)^2 lt.eq 8$, Proposition 26 gives a nonzero diagonal coefficient in Theorem 24 on a point blowup of $X_0$. If $K_(X_0)^2 = 9$, Proposition 31 gives such a coefficient on $X_0$ itself. In either case Corollary 25 and Lemma 4 yield
  $ "CH"_0 \( S \)^0 ⊗_(bb(Z)) bb(Q) = 0 . $
  Let $z in "CH"_0 \( S \)^0$. Vanishing after tensoring with $bb(Q)$ means that some nonzero integer annihilates $z$. Roitman’s torsion Theorem makes the Albanese map injective on torsion zero-cycles over $bb(C)$ [6] Theorem 4.2; see also [30]. But $"Alb" \( S \) = 0$, since $q \( S \) = 0$. Therefore $z = 0$. Thus the integral degree map is injective. Any complex point of $S$ has degree one, so it is also surjective. ◻
]

#proof[
  If $S$ is not of general type, Bloch, Kas, and Lieberman prove that the kernel of $"alb"_S$ vanishes with integral coefficients [8] pp. 135–136. Surjectivity gives the claim.
  Suppose $S$ is of general type, and let $S_0$ be a smooth minimal model. The inequalities $K_(S_0)^2 > 0$ and $deg c_2 \( S_0 \) > 0$, together with Noether’s formula, give $1 - q \( S_0 \) + p_g \( S_0 \) > 0$ [29] Remark 1.3.1. By Lemma 4, $p_g \( S_0 \) = p_g \( S \) = 0$ and $q \( S_0 \) = q \( S \)$, so the inequality forces $q \( S \) = 0$. Theorem 1 now gives $"CH"_0 \( S \)^0 = 0$, while $"Alb" \( S \) \( bb(C) \) = 0$. Thus $"alb"_S$ is an isomorphism. ◻
]

=== 8.2 Rational Chow motives <subsec:motivic-consequences>

We now pass from integral zero-cycles to rational Chow motives. Corollary 2 supplies the Albanese-kernel vanishing for every surface with $p_g = 0$; the additional hypothesis $q = 0$ enters only in the pure-Tate conclusions below.

#proof[
  Let $T \( S \)$ be the kernel of the rational Albanese map
  $ "CH"_0 \( S \)^0 ⊗_(bb(Z)) bb(Q) arrow.r "Alb" \( S \) \( bb(C) \) ⊗_(bb(Z)) bb(Q) . $
  Corollary 2 gives $T \( S \) = 0$. Kahn–Murre–Pedrini’s equivalence [22] Corollaries 7.4.9 and 7.6.11 then gives $t_2 \( S \) = 0$ and Kimura finite-dimensionality of $h \( S \)_(bb(Q))$. Their hypotheses hold over $bb(C)$, an algebraically closed characteristic-zero field of infinite transcendence degree over $bb(Q)$. This step does not require $q \( S \) = 0$.
  Now assume $q \( S \) = 0$. Kahn–Murre–Pedrini’s surface Chow–Künneth decomposition [22] Propositions 7.2.1 and 7.2.3 is
  $ h \( S \)_(bb(Q)) tilde.eq upright(bold(1)) xor h_1 \( S \) xor bb(L)^(xor rho \( S \)) xor t_2 \( S \) xor h_3 \( S \) xor bb(L)^2 \, $
  where $rho \( S \)$ is the Picard number. The endomorphism identifications for $h_1 \( S \)$ and $h_3 \( S \)$ with those of the Albanese and Picard varieties show that these two motives vanish when $q \( S \) = 0$. The Betti realization of the middle decomposition, with $t_2 \( S \) = 0$, gives $rho \( S \) = b_2 \( S \)$. This gives the asserted decomposition. Tensoring it proves the assertion for all powers of $S$.
  For a partition $nu = 1^(a_1) dots.h.c n^(a_n)$ of $n gt.eq 1$, put $ell \( nu \) = sum_j a_j$, $Sigma_nu = product_j frak(S)_(a_j)$, and $S^(\( nu \)) = S^(ell \( nu \)) \/ Sigma_nu$. The formula of de Cataldo–Migliorini [14] Theorem 6.2.1 gives
  $ h \( S^(\[ n \]) \)_(bb(Q)) tilde.eq xor.big_(nu tack.r n) h \( S^(\( nu \)) \)_(bb(Q)) ⊗ bb(L)^(thin n - ell \( nu \)) . $
  Here $h \( S^(\( nu \)) \)_(bb(Q))$ is the rational quotient motive of the possibly singular symmetric-product quotient. In the quotient formalism of [14] Section 4.3, Lemma 4.3.1, and Remark 5.4.5, it is the image on $h \( S^(ell \( nu \)) \)_(bb(Q))$ of the averaging projector
  $ e_nu = frac(1, \| Sigma_nu \|) sum_(sigma in Sigma_nu) Gamma_sigma . $
  Since $"Hom" \( bb(L)^a \, bb(L)^b \)$ is zero for $a eq.not b$ and is $bb(Q)$ for $a = b$, every idempotent summand of a finite Tate sum is again a finite Tate sum. Each term in the formula is therefore pure Tate, as is $h \( S^(\[ n \]) \)_(bb(Q))$. The case $n = 0$ is immediate.
  Finally, on a summand $bb(L)^a$, the rational Chow group and even Betti cohomology each have one generator in codimension $a$ and vanish in other codimensions; cycle class identifies these generators, and odd Betti cohomology vanishes. Compatibility of cycle class with correspondences transfers these assertions to the stated pure Tate motives. Thus the displayed cycle-class maps are isomorphisms of rational vector spaces. This uses the decomposition in rational Chow motives, not finite-dimensionality alone. ◻
]

